#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:3
msgid "Logging Information"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:10
msgid "Log Levels"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:12
msgid ""
"You can log information to the local log files by using the "
":php:func:`log_message()` method. You must supply the \"level\" of the "
"error in the first parameter, indicating what type of message it is "
"(debug, error, etc). The second parameter is the message itself:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:18
msgid ""
"There are eight different log levels, matching to the `RFC 5424 "
"<https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5424>`_ levels, and they are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:21
msgid "Level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:21
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:23
msgid "debug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:23
msgid "Detailed debug information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:24
msgid "info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:24
msgid ""
"Interesting events in your application, like a user logging in, logging "
"SQL queries, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:25
msgid "notice"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:25
msgid "Normal, but significant events in your application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:26
msgid "warning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:26
msgid ""
"Exceptional occurrences that are not errors, like the use of deprecated "
"APIs, poor use of an API, or other undesirable things that are not "
"necessarily wrong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:28
msgid "error"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:28
msgid ""
"Runtime errors that do not require immediate action but should typically "
"be logged and monitored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:29
msgid "critical"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:29
msgid ""
"Critical conditions, like an application component not available, or an "
"unexpected exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:30
msgid "alert"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:30
msgid ""
"Action must be taken immediately, like when an entire website is down, "
"the database unavailable, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:31
msgid "emergency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:31
msgid "The system is unusable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:34
msgid ""
"The logging system does not provide ways to alert sysadmins or webmasters"
" about these events, they solely log the information. For many of the "
"more critical event levels, the logging happens automatically by the "
"Error Handler, described above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:41
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:43
msgid ""
"You can modify which levels are actually logged, as well as assign "
"different Loggers to handle different levels, within the "
"**app/Config/Logger.php** configuration file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:46
msgid ""
"The ``threshold`` value of the config file determines which levels are "
"logged across your application. If any levels are requested to be logged "
"by the application, but the threshold doesn't allow them to log "
"currently, they will be ignored. The simplest method to use is to set "
"this value to the minimum level that you want to have logged. For "
"example, if you want to log warning messages, and not information "
"messages, you would set the threshold to ``5``. Any log requests with a "
"level of 5 or less (which includes runtime errors, system errors, etc) "
"would be logged and info, notices, and debug would be ignored:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:55
msgid ""
"A complete list of levels and their corresponding threshold value is in "
"the configuration file for your reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:57
msgid ""
"You can pick and choose the specific levels that you would like logged by"
" assigning an array of log level numbers to the threshold value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:63
msgid "Using Multiple Log Handlers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:65
msgid ""
"The logging system can support multiple methods of handling logging "
"running at the same time. Each handler can be set to handle specific "
"levels and ignore the rest. Currently, three handlers come with a default"
" install:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:68
msgid ""
"**File Handler** is the default handler and will create a single file for"
" every day locally. This is the recommended method of logging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:70
msgid ""
"**ChromeLogger Handler** If you have the `ChromeLogger extension "
"<https://craig.is/writing/chrome-logger>`_ installed in the Chrome web "
"browser, you can use this handler to display the log information in "
"Chrome's console window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:73
msgid ""
"**Errorlog Handler** This handler will take advantage of PHP's native "
"``error_log()`` function and write the logs there. Currently, only the "
"``0`` and ``4`` message types of ``error_log()`` are supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:76
msgid ""
"The handlers are configured in the main configuration file, in the "
"``$handlers`` property, which is simply an array of handlers and their "
"configuration. Each handler is specified with the key being the fully "
"name-spaced class name. The value will be an array of varying properties,"
" specific to each handler. Each handler's section will have one property "
"in common: ``handles``, which is an array of log level *names* that the "
"handler will log information for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:85
msgid "Modifying the Message with Context"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:87
msgid ""
"You will often want to modify the details of your message based on the "
"context of the event being logged. You might need to log a user id, an IP"
" address, the current POST variables, etc. You can do this by use "
"placeholders in your message. Each placeholder must be wrapped in curly "
"braces. In the third parameter, you must provide an array of placeholder "
"names (without the braces) and their values. These will be inserted into "
"the message string:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:95
msgid ""
"If you want to log an Exception or an Error, you can use the key of "
"'exception', and the value being the Exception or Error itself. A string "
"will be generated from that object containing the error message, the file"
" name and line number. You must still provide the exception placeholder "
"in the message:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:101
msgid ""
"Several core placeholders exist that will be automatically expanded for "
"you based on the current page request:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:104
msgid "Placeholder"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/general/logging.rst:104
msgid "Inserted value"
msgstr ""

